/*
 *  THE GROUP-BOX LOGO, ONE PER RADIO STATE:
 *      210  colour      radio On   -- the radio is live
 *      211  LIGHT grey   radio Off  -- the radio is off, and the logo says so
 *
 *  Both artworks are 17x26, centred in a 32x32 field at native pixel size rather
 *  than resampled, because a non-integer upscale of pixel art turns to mush. The
 *  mask makes the surround transparent, so what appears is the roundel alone on
 *  the platinum ground.
 *
 *  211 WAS #333333 AND IS NOW #999999, at the user's request: the dark grey read
 *  as an active-but-dim control rather than an off one. Two consequences worth
 *  knowing:
 *
 *    * The greys are snapped to the 6x6x6 system cube AT GENERATION (#333333 ->
 *      #999999, #777777 -> #CCCCCC), so the .png source and the shipped icl8
 *      agree. icl8 is the deepest a classic family goes, so these are what OS 9
 *      displays at ANY screen depth -- there is no deeper member for PlotIconID
 *      to pick, and chasing the exact source RGB with deeper artwork cannot work.
 *
 *    * #999999 has luminance 153, so NOTHING in it crosses the generator's
 *      "< 128 = ink" threshold and the 1-bit ICN# ink plane came out EMPTY. The
 *      generator now falls back to the opaque silhouette and says so. Invisible
 *      on a millions-of-colours display, which is exactly why it would have
 *      shipped unnoticed.
 *
 *  ⚠ THE TWO MASKS ARE IDENTICAL, verified at generation and re-verified after the
 *  lightening. That is what makes the swap safe: plotting one over the other
 *  overwrites every pixel the first one drew. DrawRadioLogo still erases to the
 *  group ground first, so this stays correct if the artwork is ever replaced with
 *  shapes that differ.
 *
 *  ⚠ Rez #include dependencies are NOT tracked by CMake -- this file is in the
 *  stamp target's DEPENDS in cpanel/CMakeLists.txt, or an artwork change would
 *  leave Rez skipped and the OLD icons silently shipped.
 *
 *  ⚠ The source artwork for 211 is a .PNG, not a .gif like 210. The generator
 *  reads the ALPHA channel to build the mask, and a GIF palette conversion drops
 *  alpha -- which would produce an all-opaque mask and paint a hard rectangle over
 *  the group box. Do not "tidy" it back to GIF.
 */

